11
00:00:44.047 --> 00:00:45.967
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:50.908
A story: one bad pod, forty percent of checkouts failing.

13
00:00:51.188 --> 00:00:57.567
On a Saturday evening, one of twelve order-service pods landed on a Kubernetes node with a failing disk.

14
00:00:57.847 --> 00:00:59.127
The pod didn't crash.

15
00:00:59.407 --> 00:01:04.036
Its readiness probe (GET or actuator or health) returned UP in 20 ms.

16
00:01:04.316 --> 00:01:10.268
But real requests on that pod took 4 to 9 seconds, because every write waited on the sick disk.

17
00:01:10.548 --> 00:01:17.673
The platform's gateway used round-robin across the twelve pods, so 1 in 12 requests landed on the sick pod.

18
00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:19.383
That's just 8 %.

19
00:01:19.663 --> 00:01:22.052
So why did 40 % of checkouts fail?

20
00:01:23.402 --> 00:01:28.778
A checkout makes ~6 calls through the gateway (cart, pricing, slot, order, payment...).

21
00:01:29.058 --> 00:01:36.034
The chance that at least one hits the bad pod is roughly 1 minus (11 or 12)⁶ about 41 %.

22
00:01:36.314 --> 00:01:39.770
The gateway retried timeouts, including on POST or orders.

23
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:47.837
Some retries landed on healthy pods and created duplicate orders (the idempotency key saved them, Ch 04).

24
00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:50.186
Others landed on the sick pod again.

25
00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:55.266
The bad pod's connection pool filled up, and round-robin kept sending it its full share.

26
00:01:56.616 --> 00:02:15.944
The fix had three parts: a load-balancing algorithm that notices slow backends (least-outstanding or P2C with latency weighting), passive outlier ejection (stop sending to a backend after consecutive failures), and a readiness check that tests real dependencies with a latency threshold.

27
00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:20.043
Retries were limited to idempotent methods with a retry budget.

28
00:02:21.393 --> 00:02:22.075
Quote:

29
00:02:22.355 --> 00:02:24.019
The thesis of this chapter.

30
00:02:24.299 --> 00:02:34.561
Load balancers and gateways are where every request's fate is decided: which instance, how long to wait, whether to retry, who the caller is, and how much they may send.

31
00:02:34.841 --> 00:02:38.510
Their defaults are designed for all backends are equal and healthy.

32
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.475
Production is never like that.

33
