74
00:04:31.239 --> 00:04:33.117
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

75
00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:37.332
Intuition: the billing counters at a supermarket.

76
00:04:37.612 --> 00:04:39.873
A supermarket has 10 billing counters.

77
00:04:40.153 --> 00:04:42.180
A floor manager directs customers.

78
00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:43.441
Round-robin:

79
00:04:43.721 --> 00:04:46.175
You to counter 1, you to 2, you to 3...

80
00:04:46.455 --> 00:04:54.348
Fair, until counter 4's scanner breaks and its queue grows to 30 people while the manager keeps sending one customer in ten there.

81
00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:55.823
Least-outstanding:

82
00:04:56.103 --> 00:04:58.215
Go to the counter with the shortest queue.

83
00:04:58.495 --> 00:05:02.591
The manager must look at every queue, which is expensive in a huge store.

84
00:05:02.871 --> 00:05:04.236
Power of two choices:

85
00:05:04.516 --> 00:05:07.396
Look at two random counters and pick the shorter queue.

86
00:05:07.676 --> 00:05:10.620
Almost as good as checking all of them, and much cheaper.

87
00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.127
Health check: the manager asks each cashier are you open?

88
00:05:15.407 --> 00:05:23.621
A cashier with a broken scanner still says yes (the §1.1 story), so the manager must also watch how fast queues move.

89
00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:24.946
Sticky session:

90
00:05:25.226 --> 00:05:28.426
You always go to counter 3 because your coupons are there.

91
00:05:28.706 --> 00:05:30.861
That works until counter 3 closes.

92
00:05:32.211 --> 00:05:34.280
What load balancers and proxies do.

93
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.904
Here is a quick table:

94
00:05:36.184 --> 00:05:37.528
Component, and Role.

95
00:05:37.808 --> 00:05:38.939
Load balancer:

96
00:05:39.219 --> 00:05:42.717
Distributes requests or connections across healthy instances.

97
00:05:42.997 --> 00:05:44.149
Reverse proxy:

98
00:05:44.429 --> 00:05:54.221
Sits in front of servers: terminates client connections, forwards requests, can cache, compress, rewrite and add headers (NGINX, Envoy, HAProxy).

99
00:05:54.501 --> 00:05:55.696
Forward proxy:

100
00:05:55.976 --> 00:06:00.776
Sits in front of clients for outbound traffic (egress proxy, corporate proxy).

101
00:06:01.056 --> 00:06:02.336
API gateway:

102
00:06:02.616 --> 00:06:14.179
A reverse proxy with API-aware policies: authentication, rate limiting, routing by path or version, request or response transformation, API keys, analytics.

103
00:06:14.459 --> 00:06:16.933
Service mesh sidecar or ambient proxy:

104
00:06:17.213 --> 00:06:25.384
A per-workload (or per-node) proxy for service-to-service traffic: mTLS, retries, timeouts, telemetry (Ch 17).

105
00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:29.315
L4 versus L7 load balancing.

106
00:06:29.595 --> 00:06:30.939
Here is a quick table:

107
00:06:31.219 --> 00:06:34.121
L4 (transport), and L7 (application).

108
00:06:34.401 --> 00:06:35.211
Sees.

109
00:06:35.491 --> 00:06:36.899
L4 (transport):

110
00:06:37.179 --> 00:06:39.910
IPs, ports, TCP or UDP.

111
00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:41.854
L7 (application):

112
00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:47.105
HTTP method, path, headers, cookies, gRPC service or method.

113
00:06:47.385 --> 00:06:48.387
Balances.

114
00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.075
L4 (transport):

115
00:06:50.355 --> 00:06:51.273
Connections.

116
00:06:51.553 --> 00:06:53.217
L7 (application):

117
00:06:53.497 --> 00:06:54.414
Requests.

118
00:06:54.694 --> 00:06:55.569
TLS.

119
00:06:55.849 --> 00:06:57.257
L4 (transport):

120
00:06:57.537 --> 00:06:59.115
Pass-through (or terminate).

121
00:06:59.395 --> 00:07:01.059
L7 (application):

122
00:07:01.339 --> 00:07:02.982
Terminates (or re-encrypts).

123
00:07:03.262 --> 00:07:04.094
Routing.

124
00:07:04.374 --> 00:07:05.782
L4 (transport):

125
00:07:06.062 --> 00:07:07.427
By port or IP.

126
00:07:07.707 --> 00:07:09.371
L7 (application):

127
00:07:09.651 --> 00:07:11.358
By path, host, header, weight.

128
00:07:11.638 --> 00:07:12.513
Features.

129
00:07:12.793 --> 00:07:14.201
L4 (transport):

130
00:07:14.481 --> 00:07:19.195
Very fast, protocol-agnostic, preserves the client connection end-to-end.

131
00:07:19.475 --> 00:07:21.139
L7 (application):

132
00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:26.305
Retries, timeouts, header manipulation, rate limiting, auth, canaries, compression.

133
00:07:26.585 --> 00:07:27.310
Cost.

134
00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:28.998
L4 (transport):

135
00:07:29.278 --> 00:07:31.262
Lowest latency and CPU.

136
00:07:31.542 --> 00:07:33.206
L7 (application):

137
00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:35.982
More CPU per request; more features.

138
00:07:37.332 --> 00:07:38.185
Pitfall.

139
00:07:38.465 --> 00:07:39.873
L4 (transport):

140
00:07:40.153 --> 00:07:49.284
Long-lived connections (gRPC, HTTP or 2, WebSocket) pin to one backend, so load stays uneven after scale-out.

141
00:07:49.564 --> 00:07:51.228
L7 (application):

142
00:07:51.508 --> 00:07:54.964
Buffering and timeouts affect streaming (Ch 03).

143
00:07:55.244 --> 00:07:56.204
Examples.

144
00:07:56.484 --> 00:07:57.892
L4 (transport):

145
00:07:58.172 --> 00:08:03.228
AWS NLB, GCP TCP or UDP LB, IPVS, kube-proxy.

146
00:08:03.508 --> 00:08:05.172
L7 (application):

147
00:08:05.452 --> 00:08:12.343
AWS ALB, GCP HTTP(S) LB, NGINX, Envoy, HAProxy, Spring Cloud Gateway.

148
00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:18.450
Warning: gRPC and HTTP or 2 through an L4 balancer.

149
00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:23.935
HTTP or 2 multiplexes many requests over one long-lived connection.

150
00:08:24.215 --> 00:08:33.346
An L4 balancer picks a backend once per connection, so a client with 1 connection sends 100 % of its requests to one pod forever.

151
00:08:33.626 --> 00:08:41.605
Use L7 balancing (Envoy, gRPC-aware LBs), client-side balancing, or bounded connection lifetimes.

152
00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:44.917
Load-balancing algorithms, then.

153
00:08:45.197 --> 00:08:46.541
Here is a quick table:

154
00:08:46.821 --> 00:08:49.573
Algorithm, and How, and Strengths, and Weaknesses.

155
00:08:49.853 --> 00:08:50.877
Round-robin.

156
00:08:51.157 --> 00:08:51.819
How:

157
00:08:52.099 --> 00:08:53.485
Cycle through backends.

158
00:08:53.765 --> 00:08:54.619
Strengths:

159
00:08:54.899 --> 00:08:56.712
Simple, even request counts.

160
00:08:56.992 --> 00:08:57.973
Weaknesses:

161
00:08:58.253 --> 00:09:02.349
Ignores load and speed differences; slow backends keep their full share.

162
00:09:02.629 --> 00:09:03.952
Weighted round-robin.

163
00:09:04.232 --> 00:09:04.893
How:

164
00:09:05.173 --> 00:09:06.581
Proportional to weights.

165
00:09:06.861 --> 00:09:07.715
Strengths:

166
00:09:07.995 --> 00:09:10.704
Heterogeneous instance sizes; canaries.

167
00:09:10.984 --> 00:09:11.965
Weaknesses:

168
00:09:12.245 --> 00:09:14.549
Static weights don't react to degradation.

169
00:09:14.829 --> 00:09:15.619
Random.

170
00:09:15.899 --> 00:09:16.560
How:

171
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:18.013
Uniform random.

172
00:09:18.293 --> 00:09:19.147
Strengths:

173
00:09:19.427 --> 00:09:21.304
Stateless, no coordination.

174
00:09:21.584 --> 00:09:22.565
Weaknesses:

175
00:09:22.845 --> 00:09:24.744
Same as RR; more variance.

176
00:09:25.024 --> 00:09:27.904
Least connections or least outstanding requests.

177
00:09:28.184 --> 00:09:28.845
How:

178
00:09:29.125 --> 00:09:31.429
Backend with the fewest in-flight requests.

179
00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:32.563
Strengths:

180
00:09:32.843 --> 00:09:35.061
Reacts to slow backends automatically.

181
00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:36.323
Weaknesses:

182
00:09:36.603 --> 00:09:41.680
Needs accurate counts; with many LB instances, each sees only its own traffic.

183
00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:45.206
Power of two choices (P2C).

184
00:09:45.486 --> 00:09:46.147
How:

185
00:09:46.427 --> 00:09:49.414
Pick 2 random backends, send to the less loaded.

186
00:09:49.694 --> 00:09:50.547
Strengths:

187
00:09:50.827 --> 00:09:55.777
Near least-outstanding quality, O(1), avoids herding onto one best backend.

188
00:09:56.057 --> 00:09:57.038
Weaknesses:

189
00:09:57.318 --> 00:09:59.174
Needs a load signal per backend.

190
00:09:59.454 --> 00:10:01.459
P2C + EWMA latency.

191
00:10:01.739 --> 00:10:02.401
How:

192
00:10:02.681 --> 00:10:05.710
Load = outstanding times EWMA response time.

193
00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:06.843
Strengths:

194
00:10:07.123 --> 00:10:09.214
Also avoids backends that are slow.

195
00:10:09.494 --> 00:10:10.475
Weaknesses:

196
00:10:10.755 --> 00:10:13.230
Tuning decay; needs latency tracking.

197
00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:14.683
Consistent hashing.

198
00:10:14.963 --> 00:10:15.625
How:

199
00:10:15.905 --> 00:10:18.785
Hash(key), then position on a ring, then backend.

200
00:10:19.065 --> 00:10:19.918
Strengths:

201
00:10:20.198 --> 00:10:26.747
Affinity: same key goes to the same backend (cache locality); minimal remapping when backends change.

202
00:10:27.027 --> 00:10:28.009
Weaknesses:

203
00:10:28.289 --> 00:10:32.278
Hot keys overload one backend; uneven without virtual nodes.

204
00:10:33.628 --> 00:10:35.676
Rendezvous (HRW) hashing.

205
00:10:35.956 --> 00:10:36.617
How:

206
00:10:36.897 --> 00:10:39.031
Highest hash(key, backend) wins.

207
00:10:39.311 --> 00:10:40.164
Strengths:

208
00:10:40.444 --> 00:10:42.705
Affinity like a ring, simpler, even.

209
00:10:42.985 --> 00:10:43.967
Weaknesses:

210
00:10:44.247 --> 00:10:46.337
O(n) per pick (fine for small n).

211
00:10:46.617 --> 00:10:47.769
Maglev hashing.

212
00:10:48.049 --> 00:10:48.711
How:

213
00:10:48.991 --> 00:10:51.295
Lookup table built from permutations.

214
00:10:51.575 --> 00:10:52.428
Strengths:

215
00:10:52.708 --> 00:10:54.905
Fast, even, minimal disruption.

216
00:10:55.185 --> 00:10:56.167
Weaknesses:

217
00:10:56.447 --> 00:10:57.492
More complex.

218
00:10:58.842 --> 00:11:02.362
L1's simulation (§5) shows why this matters.

219
00:11:02.642 --> 00:11:07.997
There are 10 backends, one of them 8 times slower, at 70 % fleet utilization:

220
00:11:09.347 --> 00:11:10.264
On screen:

221
00:11:10.544 --> 00:11:17.435
Algorithm p50 ms p99 ms p99.9 ms % to slow node.

222
00:11:17.715 --> 00:11:22.536
Round-robin 13.9 unbounded unbounded 10.0%.

223
00:11:22.816 --> 00:11:27.552
Random 22.5 unbounded unbounded 10.0%.

224
00:11:27.832 --> 00:11:36.579
Round-robin + slow node ejected 14.5 94.1 137.3 0.0%.

225
00:11:36.859 --> 00:11:44.944
Least-outstanding (global) 8.4 113.5 360.0 2.0%.

226
00:11:45.224 --> 00:11:53.608
Power of two choices 13.5 213.6 556.5 2.0%.

227
00:11:56.458 --> 00:12:07.039
Round-robin and random send the degraded node 10 % of traffic when it can serve only ~2 %, so its queue grows without bound and the tail latency explodes.

228
00:12:07.319 --> 00:12:11.074
Load-aware algorithms automatically cut its share to 2 %.

229
00:12:11.354 --> 00:12:14.938
Ejecting the node entirely is best of all, if you can detect it.

230
00:12:16.288 --> 00:12:16.928
Tip:

231
00:12:17.208 --> 00:12:20.259
Why P2C instead of 'always the least loaded'?.

232
00:12:20.539 --> 00:12:33.595
With many independent LB instances (every gateway pod, every client), always pick the least loaded makes them all pick the same backend at the same moment (herding), because their load information is stale.

233
00:12:33.875 --> 00:12:39.293
Choosing the better of two random candidates spreads decisions while still avoiding bad backends.

234
00:12:39.573 --> 00:12:46.101
The theory: max load drops from O(log n or log log n) to O(log log n) (Mitzenmacher).

235
00:12:47.451 --> 00:12:49.222
Consistent hashing and affinity.

236
00:12:49.502 --> 00:12:50.846
Here is the diagram.

237
00:12:54.696 --> 00:12:56.040
Here is a quick table:

238
00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.133
Use, and Why affinity helps.

239
00:12:58.413 --> 00:13:01.571
Sharded caches (clients choose the cache node by key):

240
00:13:01.851 --> 00:13:03.515
Each key lives on one node.

241
00:13:03.795 --> 00:13:07.101
Stateful in-memory processing (a per-store aggregator):

242
00:13:07.381 --> 00:13:08.683
State stays local.

243
00:13:08.963 --> 00:13:10.264
Request coalescing:

244
00:13:10.544 --> 00:13:14.533
The same key goes to the same pod, so single-flight works fleet-wide.

245
00:13:15.883 --> 00:13:23.606
When a backend is added or removed, only ~1 or n of keys move (versus almost all keys with hash % n).

246
00:13:23.886 --> 00:13:28.835
Virtual nodes (100 to 200 per backend) smooth the distribution.

247
00:13:29.115 --> 00:13:39.974
Bounded-load consistent hashing caps any backend at, say, 125 % of average load and spills the excess to the next node, which protects against hot keys.

