253
00:13:51.023 --> 00:13:55.375
LB probes GET or health every N s; unhealthy after K failures.

254
00:13:55.655 --> 00:13:56.551
Catches:

255
00:13:56.831 --> 00:13:59.988
Dead processes, crashed pods, unreachable nodes.

256
00:14:00.268 --> 00:14:01.079
Misses:

257
00:14:01.359 --> 00:14:06.927
Slow-but-alive backends (§1.1); issues that only real traffic triggers.

258
00:14:07.207 --> 00:14:09.063
Passive (outlier detection).

259
00:14:09.343 --> 00:14:10.004
How:

260
00:14:10.284 --> 00:14:17.409
Watch real responses: consecutive 5xx, timeouts, latency outliers, then eject for a period.

261
00:14:17.689 --> 00:14:18.585
Catches:

262
00:14:18.865 --> 00:14:20.956
Degraded backends under real load.

263
00:14:21.236 --> 00:14:22.047
Misses:

264
00:14:22.327 --> 00:14:23.884
Backends with no traffic.

265
00:14:24.164 --> 00:14:25.551
Kubernetes readiness.

266
00:14:25.831 --> 00:14:30.140
How: kubelet probe; failing pods are removed from Service endpoints.

267
00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:31.316
Catches:

268
00:14:31.596 --> 00:14:34.540
Startup, dependency readiness, overload shedding.

269
00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:35.631
Misses:

270
00:14:35.911 --> 00:14:39.495
Same as active, and slow to react (period times threshold).

271
00:14:40.845 --> 00:14:42.210
Kubernetes liveness.

272
00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:45.263
How: kubelet probe; failing, then restart.

273
00:14:45.543 --> 00:14:46.439
Catches:

274
00:14:46.719 --> 00:14:48.383
Deadlocks, hung processes.

275
00:14:48.663 --> 00:14:52.930
Misses: and it's dangerous if it checks dependencies (restart storms).

276
00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:55.688
Health endpoint design:

277
00:14:57.038 --> 00:14:58.382
Here is a quick table:

278
00:14:58.662 --> 00:15:03.355
Endpoint, and Checks, and Used by. /actuator or health or liveness.

279
00:15:03.635 --> 00:15:04.361
Checks:

280
00:15:04.641 --> 00:15:06.667
Process can serve (no deadlock).

281
00:15:06.947 --> 00:15:08.803
Used by: kubelet liveness.

282
00:15:09.083 --> 00:15:14.225
Never checks the DB or downstreams. /actuator or health or readiness.

283
00:15:14.505 --> 00:15:15.230
Checks:

284
00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:21.739
Warm-up done, critical dependencies reachable, not overloaded (for example, the pool isn't saturated).

285
00:15:22.019 --> 00:15:24.345
Used by: kubelet readiness, LB.

286
00:15:24.625 --> 00:15:25.969
Deep diagnostics.

287
00:15:26.249 --> 00:15:26.974
Checks:

288
00:15:27.254 --> 00:15:29.195
Every dependency with latency.

289
00:15:29.475 --> 00:15:30.393
Used by:

290
00:15:30.673 --> 00:15:32.422
Humans and dashboards only.

291
00:15:32.702 --> 00:15:35.091
It's expensive and must not gate traffic.

292
00:15:36.441 --> 00:15:37.359
Watch out:

293
00:15:37.639 --> 00:15:41.116
Health checks that check dependencies can take down the whole fleet.

294
00:15:41.396 --> 00:15:46.751
If every pod's readiness depends on Redis, a Redis blip makes all pods unready at once.

295
00:15:47.031 --> 00:15:52.151
The LB then has zero backends, so a partial degradation becomes a total outage.

296
00:15:52.431 --> 00:15:56.249
Readiness should reflect whether this instance can serve useful traffic.

297
00:15:56.529 --> 00:16:04.529
Shared-dependency outages are handled by fallbacks and shedding (Ch 07, 18), not by removing every pod.

298
00:16:05.879 --> 00:16:26.893
Outlier ejection parameters (Envoy-style): eject after 5 consecutive 5xx or gateway errors, eject for 30 s (growing with repeated ejections), never eject more than 50 % of the pool (max ejection percent, which keeps a correlated failure from emptying the pool), and return the backend on probation.

299
00:16:28.243 --> 00:16:30.248
Sticky sessions (session affinity).

300
00:16:30.528 --> 00:16:35.648
Sticky sessions route a user to the same backend (via a cookie or source-IP hash).

301
00:16:36.998 --> 00:16:38.342
Here is a quick table:

302
00:16:38.622 --> 00:16:39.838
Problem, and Why.

303
00:16:40.118 --> 00:16:41.185
Uneven load:

304
00:16:41.465 --> 00:16:43.299
Heavy users pin to one pod.

305
00:16:43.579 --> 00:16:45.627
Deploys and scale-in drop sessions:

306
00:16:45.907 --> 00:16:47.763
Session state dies with the pod.

307
00:16:48.043 --> 00:16:49.494
Failover loses state:

308
00:16:49.774 --> 00:16:50.478
Same.

309
00:16:50.758 --> 00:16:53.318
Autoscaling doesn't help existing users:

310
00:16:53.598 --> 00:16:55.070
They stay on hot pods.

311
00:16:56.420 --> 00:17:05.145
Prefer stateless services: keep session state in a shared store (Redis sessions, JWT claims) so any pod can serve any request.

312
00:17:05.425 --> 00:17:11.527
Use affinity only for performance (cache locality via consistent hashing), never for correctness.

313
00:17:11.807 --> 00:17:20.169
Streaming connections (Ch 03) are the exception, and even there, bounded lifetimes and resume make stickiness unnecessary.

314
00:17:21.519 --> 00:17:24.143
Retries, timeouts and hedging at the proxy.

315
00:17:24.423 --> 00:17:25.767
Here is a quick table:

316
00:17:26.047 --> 00:17:27.135
Rule, and Why.

317
00:17:27.415 --> 00:17:33.154
Retry only idempotent requests (GET, PUT, DELETE, or POST with an idempotency key):

318
00:17:33.434 --> 00:17:38.981
Otherwise you get duplicate orders or payments (§1.1, Ch 04).

319
00:17:39.261 --> 00:17:40.967
Retry on a different backend:

320
00:17:41.247 --> 00:17:43.850
The same sick backend usually fails again.

321
00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:51.618
Retry budget (for example, retries at most 10 to 20 % of requests) instead of per-request retry counts only:

322
00:17:51.898 --> 00:17:59.535
Prevents retry storms from multiplying load during outages (3 layers times 3 retries = 27 times ).

323
00:17:59.815 --> 00:18:01.159
Retry at one layer:

324
00:18:01.439 --> 00:18:05.173
Gateway, mesh and client all retrying multiplies attempts.

325
00:18:05.453 --> 00:18:07.629
Per-route timeouts from latency budgets:

326
00:18:07.909 --> 00:18:13.882
Search 800 ms, checkout 3 s, streams: no response timeout (Ch 03).

327
00:18:15.232 --> 00:18:20.864
Hedged requests (send a second copy after the p95 delay, take the first response):

328
00:18:21.144 --> 00:18:25.283
Cuts tail latency for idempotent reads at a small load cost.

329
00:18:25.563 --> 00:18:26.587
Jittered backoff:

330
00:18:26.867 --> 00:18:28.915
Avoids synchronized retry waves.

331
00:18:30.265 --> 00:18:32.334
API gateway responsibilities.

332
00:18:32.614 --> 00:18:33.958
Here is a quick table:

333
00:18:34.238 --> 00:18:36.670
Responsibility, and Gateway, and Service.

334
00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.385
TLS termination (behind the LB or CDN).

335
00:18:40.665 --> 00:18:41.561
Gateway:

336
00:18:41.841 --> 00:18:42.886
(or the LB).

337
00:18:43.166 --> 00:18:45.193
Service: mTLS in the mesh.

338
00:18:45.473 --> 00:18:49.419
Authentication (validate JWT or API key).

339
00:18:49.699 --> 00:18:50.531
Service:

340
00:18:50.811 --> 00:18:54.886
Re-check critical claims; trust only gateway-set identity headers.

341
00:18:55.166 --> 00:18:57.875
Coarse authorization (scopes: orders:read).

342
00:18:58.155 --> 00:18:58.987
Service:

343
00:18:59.267 --> 00:19:02.339
Fine-grained (is this my order?) always in the service.

344
00:19:02.619 --> 00:19:04.155
Rate limiting and quotas.

345
00:19:04.435 --> 00:19:06.249
Gateway: per client or plan.

346
00:19:06.529 --> 00:19:07.361
Service:

347
00:19:07.641 --> 00:19:09.795
Service-level concurrency limits too.

348
00:19:10.075 --> 00:19:12.379
Routing (path, version, canary weights).

349
00:19:13.729 --> 00:19:17.847
Request size limits, header normalization, stripping spoofable headers.

350
00:19:18.127 --> 00:19:19.876
Correlation ID injection.

351
00:19:20.156 --> 00:19:20.988
Service:

352
00:19:21.268 --> 00:19:22.185
Propagate.

353
00:19:22.465 --> 00:19:24.129
Response compression, CORS.

354
00:19:24.409 --> 00:19:27.311
Timeouts, retries (idempotent), circuit breaking.

355
00:19:27.591 --> 00:19:28.423
Service:

356
00:19:28.703 --> 00:19:30.111
Downstream calls too.

357
00:19:30.391 --> 00:19:32.545
Request or response transformation.

358
00:19:32.825 --> 00:19:33.721
Gateway:

359
00:19:34.001 --> 00:19:35.580
Minimal (versioning shims).

360
00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:37.780
Business logic, orchestration.

361
00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:38.892
Service:

362
00:19:39.172 --> 00:19:40.729
BFFs and services.

363
00:19:42.079 --> 00:19:42.911
Caching.

364
00:19:43.191 --> 00:19:44.087
Gateway:

365
00:19:44.367 --> 00:19:45.946
Rarely (anonymous GETs).

366
00:19:46.226 --> 00:19:47.058
Service:

367
00:19:47.338 --> 00:19:48.725
Ch 07.

368
00:19:50.075 --> 00:19:50.992
Watch out:

369
00:19:51.272 --> 00:19:52.765
Identity header spoofing.

370
00:19:53.045 --> 00:20:04.288
If services trust X-User-Id set by the gateway, the gateway must remove any incoming X-User-Id from the client before setting its own from the validated JWT.

371
00:20:04.568 --> 00:20:08.493
Otherwise any client can impersonate any user by sending the header.

372
00:20:08.773 --> 00:20:17.456
Also ensure services are unreachable except through the gateway or mesh (NetworkPolicies), or re-validate the token in the service.

373
00:20:18.806 --> 00:20:20.214
Spring Cloud Gateway.

374
00:20:20.494 --> 00:20:21.838
Here is a quick table:

375
00:20:22.118 --> 00:20:23.419
Concept, and Meaning.

376
00:20:23.699 --> 00:20:32.809
Route: id + predicates + filters + destination URI (lb://order-service uses Spring Cloud LoadBalancer).

377
00:20:33.089 --> 00:20:34.006
Predicates:

378
00:20:34.286 --> 00:20:39.278
Path, Host, Method, Header, Query, Weight (canary), Before or After or Between (time).

379
00:20:39.558 --> 00:20:40.454
Filters:

380
00:20:40.734 --> 00:20:52.275
Add or remove headers, RewritePath, StripPrefix, RequestRateLimiter (Redis), CircuitBreaker (Resilience4j), Retry, RequestSize, SetStatus, custom GlobalFilters.

381
00:20:52.555 --> 00:20:53.430
Flavours:

382
00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:57.721
Reactive (WebFlux, Netty) is the mature, highest-throughput option.

383
00:20:58.001 --> 00:21:03.611
Server WebMVC (servlet) is simpler to reason about and works well with virtual threads.

384
00:21:03.891 --> 00:21:05.001
Per-route timeouts:

385
00:21:05.281 --> 00:21:10.593
Route metadata connect-timeout, response-timeout (-1 disables for streams).

386
00:21:11.943 --> 00:21:12.967
Alternatives:

387
00:21:13.247 --> 00:21:21.481
Envoy-based gateways (Envoy Gateway, Istio ingress), Kong, NGINX or OpenResty, and cloud API gateways.

388
00:21:21.761 --> 00:21:28.396
Choose Spring Cloud Gateway when the team wants Java-coded policies and Spring ecosystem integration.

389
00:21:28.676 --> 00:21:32.985
Choose Envoy-based options for polyglot platforms and mesh alignment.

390
00:21:34.335 --> 00:21:35.893
Global traffic management:

391
00:21:36.173 --> 00:21:38.519
GeoDNS, anycast, failover.

392
00:21:38.799 --> 00:21:40.143
Here is a quick table:

393
00:21:40.423 --> 00:21:42.813
Technique, and How, and Pros, and Cons.

394
00:21:43.093 --> 00:21:45.674
GeoDNS or latency-based DNS.

395
00:21:45.954 --> 00:21:46.615
How:

396
00:21:46.895 --> 00:21:51.695
DNS answers depend on the resolver's location or measured latency.

397
00:21:51.975 --> 00:21:52.829
Pros:

398
00:21:53.109 --> 00:21:54.965
Simple, works with any backend.

399
00:21:55.245 --> 00:21:56.055
Cons:

400
00:21:56.335 --> 00:22:03.055
Resolver location is not user location; clients cache beyond TTL, so failover takes minutes.

401
00:22:03.335 --> 00:22:04.594
DNS failover.

402
00:22:04.874 --> 00:22:05.535
How:

403
00:22:05.815 --> 00:22:08.802
Health-checked records; answer only healthy regions.

404
00:22:09.082 --> 00:22:09.935
Pros:

405
00:22:10.215 --> 00:22:11.709
Cheap regional DR.

406
00:22:11.989 --> 00:22:12.799
Cons:

407
00:22:13.079 --> 00:22:17.047
Slow (TTL + client caching); partial failures are hard.

408
00:22:17.327 --> 00:22:18.202
Anycast.

409
00:22:18.482 --> 00:22:19.143
How:

410
00:22:19.423 --> 00:22:24.821
The same IP announced from many PoPs, and BGP routes users to the nearest.

411
00:22:25.101 --> 00:22:25.954
Pros:

412
00:22:26.234 --> 00:22:31.674
Fast failover (routing converges in seconds), DDoS absorption, one IP.

413
00:22:31.954 --> 00:22:32.765
Cons:

414
00:22:33.045 --> 00:22:40.618
Needs a provider (CDNs, global LBs); TCP sessions can break on route changes (QUIC handles it better).

415
00:22:41.968 --> 00:22:46.661
Global L7 LB (cloud global LBs, CDN load balancing).

416
00:22:46.941 --> 00:22:47.603
How:

417
00:22:47.883 --> 00:22:54.453
Anycast edge terminates TLS, then routes to regional backends by health, latency and capacity.

418
00:22:54.733 --> 00:22:55.587
Pros:

419
00:22:55.867 --> 00:22:59.536
Best user experience, fast failover, per-request decisions.

420
00:22:59.816 --> 00:23:00.627
Cons:

421
00:23:00.907 --> 00:23:02.549
Provider lock-in; cost.

422
00:23:03.899 --> 00:23:24.166
For this platform (India, one primary region + DR, Ch 27): anycast CDN or WAF at the edge terminates TLS close to users across Indian PoPs, then routes to the Mumbai region's L7 LB, with a health-checked failover origin in the DR region (Hyderabad).

423
00:23:24.446 --> 00:23:29.054
DNS points at the CDN, so DNS TTLs don't govern failover.

424
00:23:29.334 --> 00:23:31.318
The edge's origin health checks do.

425
00:23:32.668 --> 00:23:35.185
Zone-aware balancing and cross-AZ cost.

426
00:23:35.465 --> 00:23:41.353
Cross-AZ traffic adds ~0.5 to 2 ms per hop and is billed per GB.

427
00:23:41.633 --> 00:23:49.249
Zone-aware balancing prefers same-zone backends when they're healthy and not overloaded, and spills to other zones when needed.

428
00:23:49.529 --> 00:23:54.436
Envoy, Istio and Kubernetes topology-aware routing (trafficDistribution:

429
00:23:54.716 --> 00:23:56.913
PreferClose on Services) support it.

430
00:23:58.263 --> 00:23:59.607
Here is a quick table:

431
00:23:59.887 --> 00:24:01.210
Guardrail, and Why.

432
00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:06.226
Spill over when the local zone is below ~70 % healthy capacity:

433
00:24:06.506 --> 00:24:09.429
Don't overload the local zone just to save cost.

434
00:24:09.709 --> 00:24:11.607
Keep capacity balanced per zone:

435
00:24:11.887 --> 00:24:14.746
Otherwise zone-local routing concentrates load.

436
00:24:15.026 --> 00:24:17.458
Zone failure, then automatic cross-zone:

437
00:24:17.738 --> 00:24:19.295
Availability beats cost.

438
00:24:20.645 --> 00:24:21.541
Part 3.

439
00:24:21.821 --> 00:24:23.549
Architecture and Diagrams.

440
00:24:24.799 --> 00:24:27.530
Gateway internals: a request through filters.

441
00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:29.154
Here is the diagram.

442
00:24:33.004 --> 00:24:36.588
Sequence: happy path, authenticated request through the edge.

443
00:24:36.868 --> 00:24:39.663
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

444
00:24:39.943 --> 00:24:41.159
App to CDN:

445
00:24:41.439 --> 00:24:48.756
GET or api or v1 or orders?limit=20 (TLS 1.3, HTTP or 3).

446
00:24:49.036 --> 00:24:51.959
CDN to LB: forward (origin pool:

447
00:24:52.239 --> 00:24:54.031
Mumbai), X-Forwarded-For.

448
00:24:54.311 --> 00:24:58.428
LB to GW: forward to healthy gateway pod (AZ-b).

449
00:24:58.708 --> 00:25:09.033
GW to GW: strip client X-User-Id, validate JWT (cached JWKS), set X-User-Id=cust-42.

450
00:25:09.313 --> 00:25:13.516
GW to R: token bucket for cust-42 (1 Lua call).

451
00:25:13.796 --> 00:25:16.783
R to GW: allowed (remaining 17).

452
00:25:17.063 --> 00:25:18.684
GW to GW:

453
00:25:18.964 --> 00:25:27.369
P2C: pod 7 (2 in-flight, EWMA 18 ms) versus pod 3 (5 in-flight, 22 ms), then pod 7.

454
00:25:27.649 --> 00:25:29.057
GW to OS:

455
00:25:29.337 --> 00:25:34.457
GET or api or v1 or orders (timeout 2 s, X-Correlation-Id).

456
00:25:34.737 --> 00:25:36.188
OS to GW:

457
00:25:36.468 --> 00:25:37.385
200.

458
00:25:37.665 --> 00:25:38.903
GW to App:

459
00:25:39.183 --> 00:25:41.252
200 (RateLimit-Remaining:

460
00:25:41.532 --> 00:25:42.513
17).

461
00:25:46.363 --> 00:25:52.593
Sequence: failure path, slow pod detected, ejected, and a non-idempotent retry prevented.

462
00:25:52.873 --> 00:25:55.667
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

463
00:25:55.947 --> 00:25:57.441
GW to P5:

464
00:25:57.721 --> 00:26:02.521
GET or orders or ord1 (P2C picked 5: low in-flight so far).

465
00:26:02.801 --> 00:26:04.379
P5 to GW:

466
00:26:04.659 --> 00:26:08.350
200 after 4.2 s (EWMA for pod 5 jumps).

467
00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:10.123
GW to P5:

468
00:26:10.403 --> 00:26:13.390
POST or orders (rare pick), timeout 3 s.

469
00:26:13.670 --> 00:26:15.867
P5 to GW: timeout.

470
00:26:16.147 --> 00:26:17.641
GW to P5:

471
00:26:17.921 --> 00:26:19.606
GET or orders or ord2.

472
00:26:19.886 --> 00:26:23.811
P5 to GW: timeout (3rd consecutive failure).

473
00:26:24.091 --> 00:26:31.814
GW to GW: outlier ejection: pod 5 out for 30 s (max 50 % of pool may be ejected).

474
00:26:32.094 --> 00:26:36.894
GW to P2: retry GET on a different instance (within retry budget).

475
00:26:37.174 --> 00:26:38.753
P2 to GW:

476
00:26:39.033 --> 00:26:39.950
200.

477
00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:44.547
Note:

478
00:26:44.827 --> 00:26:47.600
What the system guarantees after this failure path.

479
00:26:47.880 --> 00:27:01.981
A slow-but-alive backend receives a rapidly shrinking share of traffic (latency-weighted P2C) and is removed after consecutive failures (passive ejection), without waiting for active health checks that report it as UP.

480
00:27:02.261 --> 00:27:10.005
Retries never re-send non-idempotent requests, always go to a different instance, and are capped by a fleet-wide budget.

481
00:27:10.285 --> 00:27:15.747
Ejection never removes more than half the pool, so a shared-dependency failure can't empty it.

482
00:27:17.097 --> 00:27:19.635
State: backend endpoint health in the balancer.

483
00:27:19.915 --> 00:27:21.259
Here is the diagram.

