Guidance:

994
00:57:53.809 --> 00:57:56.135
Separate pools per upstream to isolate.

995
00:57:57.485 --> 00:57:58.487
Health checks.

996
00:57:58.767 --> 00:57:59.535
Value:

997
00:57:59.815 --> 00:58:02.674
5 to 10 s interval, 2 to 3 failures.

998
00:58:02.954 --> 00:58:03.850
Guidance:

999
00:58:04.130 --> 00:58:05.517
Plus passive ejection.

1000
00:58:05.797 --> 00:58:06.885
Gateway pods.

1001
00:58:07.165 --> 00:58:07.933
Value:

1002
00:58:08.213 --> 00:58:12.202
Pre-scaled for events; target 50 to 60 % CPU.

1003
00:58:12.482 --> 00:58:13.378
Guidance:

1004
00:58:13.658 --> 00:58:16.645
Event-loop latency is the key metric (reactive).

1005
00:58:16.925 --> 00:58:20.765
Max connection age (HTTP or 2, gRPC).

1006
00:58:21.045 --> 00:58:21.813
Value:

1007
00:58:22.093 --> 00:58:23.309
5 to 10 min.

1008
00:58:23.589 --> 00:58:24.485
Guidance:

1009
00:58:24.765 --> 00:58:26.877
Rebalances long-lived connections.

1010
00:58:28.227 --> 00:58:28.952
Cost.

1011
00:58:29.232 --> 00:58:36.251
Managed LBs and gateways charge per hour, per new connection, per active connection and per GB processed.

1012
00:58:36.531 --> 00:58:46.429
At 120 K req per second, managed per-request API gateway pricing can exceed the cost of running your own gateway pods by a large factor.

1013
00:58:46.709 --> 00:58:48.501
Model it (Ch 02).

1014
00:58:48.781 --> 00:58:53.475
Cross-AZ traffic between LB, gateway and services is billed per GB.

1015
00:58:53.755 --> 00:58:59.131
Zone-aware routing reduces it, but only with the load checks from §2.12.

1016
00:58:59.411 --> 00:59:06.088
CDN offload (Ch 07) reduces origin LB and gateway capacity directly.

1017
00:59:06.368 --> 00:59:09.717
WAF and bot management are priced per request.

1018
00:59:09.997 --> 00:59:13.816
Filter the worst traffic at the edge before it costs gateway CPU.

1019
00:59:15.166 --> 00:59:16.105
Part 7.

1020
00:59:16.385 --> 00:59:18.091
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1021
00:59:19.341 --> 00:59:19.981
1.

1022
00:59:20.261 --> 00:59:22.267
Round-robin with shallow health checks.

1023
00:59:22.547 --> 00:59:23.357
Symptom:

1024
00:59:23.637 --> 00:59:29.909
A slow pod keeps its full share, and multi-call journeys fail disproportionately (§1.1).

1025
00:59:30.189 --> 00:59:30.915
Fix:

1026
00:59:31.195 --> 00:59:35.867
Latency-aware P2C plus passive ejection plus meaningful readiness.

1027
00:59:36.147 --> 00:59:36.851
2.

1028
00:59:37.131 --> 00:59:40.011
Readiness or liveness checking shared dependencies.

1029
00:59:41.361 --> 00:59:43.451
Here is the YAML code. before:

1030
00:59:43.731 --> 00:59:54.419
Redis blip, then every pod unready (or restarted) at once, then zero backends. after: instance-local readiness; shared deps handled by fallbacks or breakers.

1031
00:59:57.269 --> 00:59:58.016
3.

1032
00:59:58.296 --> 01:00:01.197
Retrying non-idempotent requests at the proxy.

1033
01:00:01.477 --> 01:00:02.288
Symptom:

1034
01:00:02.568 --> 01:00:04.659
Duplicate orders during slowdowns.

1035
01:00:04.939 --> 01:00:05.664
Fix:

1036
01:00:05.944 --> 01:00:08.056
GET or idempotent-only retries.

1037
01:00:08.336 --> 01:00:11.429
Writes are retried by clients with Idempotency-Key.

1038
01:00:11.709 --> 01:00:12.413
4.

1039
01:00:12.693 --> 01:00:14.165
Retries at every layer.

1040
01:00:14.445 --> 01:00:15.256
Symptom:

1041
01:00:15.536 --> 01:00:23.024
Client 3 times gateway 3 times mesh 3 times SDK 3 = 81 attempts per click during an outage.

1042
01:00:23.304 --> 01:00:24.029
Fix:

1043
01:00:24.309 --> 01:00:27.275
One retrying layer, budgets and circuit breakers.

1044
01:00:27.555 --> 01:00:28.323
5.

1045
01:00:28.603 --> 01:00:30.117
Sticky sessions for state.

1046
01:00:30.397 --> 01:00:31.208
Symptom:

1047
01:00:31.488 --> 01:00:34.005
Uneven load, and lost carts on deploys.

1048
01:00:34.285 --> 01:00:35.011
Fix:

1049
01:00:35.291 --> 01:00:37.851
Stateless services with a shared session store.

1050
01:00:38.131 --> 01:00:38.877
6.

1051
01:00:39.157 --> 01:00:41.845
Trusting client identity or IP headers.

1052
01:00:43.195 --> 01:01:00.603
Here is the Java code. before: the service trusts whatever X-User-Id arrives (reachable directly, or not stripped at gateway). after: gateway strips + sets from validated JWT; NetworkPolicy blocks direct access; service checks ownership.

1053
01:01:03.453 --> 01:01:04.200
7.

1054
01:01:04.480 --> 01:01:06.123
One timeout for all routes.

1055
01:01:06.403 --> 01:01:07.213
Symptom:

1056
01:01:07.493 --> 01:01:12.592
SSE streams die at 30 s, while checkout waits 30 s on a dead dependency.

1057
01:01:12.872 --> 01:01:13.597
Fix:

1058
01:01:13.877 --> 01:01:17.440
Per-route timeouts from latency budgets, with streams exempted.

1059
01:01:17.720 --> 01:01:18.403
8.

1060
01:01:18.683 --> 01:01:20.837
Zone affinity without load awareness.

1061
01:01:21.117 --> 01:01:21.928
Symptom:

1062
01:01:22.208 --> 01:01:26.091
All traffic from one zone's callers piles onto that zone's pods.

1063
01:01:26.371 --> 01:01:31.043
The first L4 simulation reproduced this: p50 150 s.

1064
01:01:31.323 --> 01:01:32.048
Fix:

1065
01:01:32.328 --> 01:01:37.789
Spill over when the local zone is busier than the fleet, or when too few local pods are healthy.

1066
01:01:39.139 --> 01:01:39.886
9.

1067
01:01:40.166 --> 01:01:41.958
Business logic in the gateway.

1068
01:01:42.238 --> 01:01:43.049
Symptom:

1069
01:01:43.329 --> 01:01:47.382
The gateway becomes a deploy bottleneck and a distributed monolith.

1070
01:01:47.662 --> 01:01:48.387
Fix:

1071
01:01:48.667 --> 01:01:50.907
Gateway for cross-cutting policy only.

1072
01:01:51.187 --> 01:01:55.241
Composition goes in BFFs, and domain rules in services.

1073
01:01:55.521 --> 01:01:59.041
10. gRPC behind an L4 load balancer.

1074
01:01:59.321 --> 01:02:00.131
Symptom:

1075
01:02:00.411 --> 01:02:02.822
New pods get no traffic after scale-out.

1076
01:02:03.102 --> 01:02:03.827
Fix:

1077
01:02:04.107 --> 01:02:09.953
An L7 or gRPC-aware balancer, client-side LB, and max connection age.

1078
01:02:11.303 --> 01:02:12.177
Part 8.

1079
01:02:12.457 --> 01:02:13.801
Interview Q and A.

1080
01:02:15.051 --> 01:02:15.883
Beginner.

1081
01:02:16.163 --> 01:02:17.187
Question 1.

1082
01:02:17.467 --> 01:02:22.523
What's the difference between a load balancer, a reverse proxy and an API gateway?

1083
01:02:22.803 --> 01:02:24.169
Thirty-second answer:

1084
01:02:24.449 --> 01:02:27.670
A load balancer distributes traffic across instances.

1085
01:02:27.950 --> 01:02:35.225
A reverse proxy fronts servers, terminating client connections and forwarding requests (and can balance, cache, compress).

1086
01:02:35.505 --> 01:02:46.406
An API gateway is a reverse proxy with API-specific policies: authentication, rate limiting, routing by path and version, quotas and transformations.

1087
01:02:47.756 --> 01:02:48.759
Question 2.

1088
01:02:49.039 --> 01:02:51.620
L4 versus L7 load balancing?

1089
01:02:51.900 --> 01:02:53.265
Thirty-second answer:

1090
01:02:53.545 --> 01:02:59.007
L4 balances connections based on IP and port, with no HTTP awareness.

1091
01:02:59.287 --> 01:03:01.335
It's fast and protocol-agnostic.

1092
01:03:01.615 --> 01:03:13.391
L7 understands HTTP or gRPC and balances individual requests, enabling path routing, retries, header manipulation and auth, at more CPU cost.

1093
01:03:14.741 --> 01:03:15.786
Question 3.

1094
01:03:16.066 --> 01:03:19.074
Name load-balancing algorithms and when each fits.

1095
01:03:19.354 --> 01:03:20.719
Thirty-second answer:

1096
01:03:20.999 --> 01:03:41.522
Round-robin and weighted round-robin (equal or proportional capacity), random, least connections or least outstanding (variable request cost), power of two choices (load-aware and scalable), P2C with latency EWMA (also avoids slow nodes), and consistent or rendezvous hashing (affinity for caches or state).

1097
01:03:42.872 --> 01:03:43.939
Question 4.

1098
01:03:44.219 --> 01:03:47.269
Liveness versus readiness probes: what should each check?

1099
01:03:47.549 --> 01:03:48.915
Thirty-second answer:

1100
01:03:49.195 --> 01:03:51.307
Liveness answers is the process stuck?

1101
01:03:51.587 --> 01:03:52.589
Restart me.

1102
01:03:52.869 --> 01:03:56.027
It should check only internal health, never dependencies.

1103
01:03:56.307 --> 01:03:58.931
Readiness answers should I receive traffic now?

1104
01:03:59.211 --> 01:04:04.117
It checks warm-up and instance-local ability to serve, possibly including overload.

1105
01:04:04.397 --> 01:04:07.960
Shared dependencies shouldn't flip readiness for all pods at once.

1106
01:04:09.310 --> 01:04:10.313
Intermediate.

1107
01:04:10.593 --> 01:04:11.702
Question 5.

1108
01:04:11.982 --> 01:04:15.118
Why avoid sticky sessions, and when are they acceptable?

1109
01:04:15.398 --> 01:04:16.763
Thirty-second answer:

1110
01:04:17.043 --> 01:04:24.766
They create uneven load, lose state on deploys, scale-in or failures, and stop autoscaling from helping existing users.

1111
01:04:25.046 --> 01:04:27.755
Make services stateless with shared session stores.

1112
01:04:28.035 --> 01:04:34.713
Affinity is acceptable for performance (cache locality via consistent hashing) when losing it is harmless.

1113
01:04:36.063 --> 01:04:37.172
Question 6.

1114
01:04:37.452 --> 01:04:40.887
Explain consistent hashing and why virtual nodes are needed.

1115
01:04:41.167 --> 01:04:42.532
Thirty-second answer:

1116
01:04:42.812 --> 01:04:47.761
Keys and servers hash onto a ring, and each key goes to the next server clockwise.

1117
01:04:48.041 --> 01:04:55.785
Adding or removing a server moves only ~1 or n of keys, unlike hash % n, which moves almost all of them.

1118
01:04:56.065 --> 01:05:01.783
Virtual nodes (many ring points per server) smooth the distribution and let you weight servers.

1119
01:05:03.133 --> 01:05:04.221
Question 7.

1120
01:05:04.501 --> 01:05:07.338
How do you configure retries at a gateway safely?

1121
01:05:07.618 --> 01:05:08.983
Thirty-second answer:

1122
01:05:09.263 --> 01:05:24.325
Only for idempotent requests (GET, PUT, DELETE, or POST with an idempotency key), on a different instance, at most once or twice with jittered backoff, within a fleet-wide retry budget, at a single layer, and never beyond the caller's deadline.

1123
01:05:24.605 --> 01:05:26.055
Pair with circuit breakers.

1124
01:05:27.405 --> 01:05:28.429
Question 8.

1125
01:05:28.709 --> 01:05:32.144
What belongs in the API gateway versus in the services?

1126
01:05:32.424 --> 01:05:33.789
Thirty-second answer:

1127
01:05:34.069 --> 01:05:34.965
Gateway:

1128
01:05:35.245 --> 01:05:51.139
TLS termination (or the LB), authentication, coarse authorization (scopes), rate limits and quotas, routing and versioning, size limits, header hygiene, correlation IDs, CORS, compression, timeouts, idempotent retries, circuit breaking.

1129
01:05:51.419 --> 01:05:58.224
Services: fine-grained authorization (ownership), business rules, data access, orchestration (or BFFs).

1130
01:05:59.574 --> 01:06:00.641
Question 9.

1131
01:06:00.921 --> 01:06:03.737
How do you load balance gRPC services?

1132
01:06:04.017 --> 01:06:12.145
Thirty-second answer: gRPC uses long-lived HTTP or 2 connections, so L4 balancing pins traffic.

1133
01:06:12.425 --> 01:06:28.830
Use L7 (Envoy or mesh) per-request balancing, or client-side balancing (gRPC name resolution + a roundrobin or custom policy, look-aside LB or xDS), plus max connection age so clients reconnect and rebalance.

1134
01:06:30.180 --> 01:06:30.948
Senior.

1135
01:06:31.228 --> 01:06:32.273
Question 10.

1136
01:06:32.553 --> 01:06:37.545
One of twelve pods is slow but passes health checks, and 40 % of checkouts fail.

1137
01:06:37.825 --> 01:06:39.063
Explain and fix it.

1138
01:06:39.343 --> 01:06:40.708
Thirty-second answer:

1139
01:06:40.988 --> 01:06:50.631
Each checkout makes ~6 calls, so P(at least one hits the bad pod) about 1 minus (11 or 12)⁶ about 41 %.

1140
01:06:50.911 --> 01:06:56.095
Round-robin keeps sending it 1 or 12 of traffic, and shallow health checks don't notice.

1141
01:06:56.375 --> 01:07:09.857
Fix it with latency-aware balancing (P2C with EWMA), passive outlier ejection, readiness reflecting real capacity, idempotent-only retries on different instances, and per-instance latency alerts.

1142
01:07:11.207 --> 01:07:12.338
Question 11.

1143
01:07:12.618 --> 01:07:16.607
Why P2C instead of always choosing the least-loaded backend?

1144
01:07:16.887 --> 01:07:18.253
Thirty-second answer:

1145
01:07:18.533 --> 01:07:29.861
With many independent balancers and slightly stale load data, always least loaded makes every balancer pick the same backend simultaneously (herding), which then overloads.

1146
01:07:30.141 --> 01:07:33.213
P2C compares only two random candidates.

1147
01:07:33.493 --> 01:07:40.938
That's almost as good at avoiding bad backends, spreads choices, is O(1), and is robust to stale information.

1148
01:07:42.288 --> 01:07:43.376
Question 12.

1149
01:07:43.656 --> 01:07:45.405
Design outlier ejection.

1150
01:07:45.685 --> 01:07:46.752
What can go wrong?

1151
01:07:47.032 --> 01:07:48.397
Thirty-second answer:

1152
01:07:48.677 --> 01:07:50.704
Watch real responses per instance.

1153
01:07:50.984 --> 01:07:56.851
Eject after N consecutive errors or when latency or success rate deviates from peers.

1154
01:07:57.131 --> 01:08:05.536
Eject for a period that grows on repeated ejection, return on probation, and cap the ejected fraction (for example 50 %).

1155
01:08:06.886 --> 01:08:08.166
Question 13.

1156
01:08:08.446 --> 01:08:12.350
How do you prevent identity spoofing between the gateway and services?

1157
01:08:12.630 --> 01:08:13.995
Thirty-second answer:

1158
01:08:14.275 --> 01:08:21.742
The gateway strips client-supplied identity and internal headers, validates the token, and sets identity headers itself.

1159
01:08:22.022 --> 01:08:29.254
Services are unreachable except through the gateway or mesh (NetworkPolicy + mTLS identities).

1160
01:08:29.534 --> 01:08:31.646
Services still check resource ownership.

1161
01:08:31.926 --> 01:08:36.982
Alternatively, forward the original JWT and have services validate it.

1162
01:08:38.332 --> 01:08:39.527
Question 14.

1163
01:08:39.807 --> 01:08:43.732
Implement distributed rate limiting at the gateway: design choices?

1164
01:08:44.012 --> 01:08:45.377
Thirty-second answer:

1165
01:08:45.657 --> 01:08:56.772
A token bucket per key (user, API key, IP range) in Redis via an atomic Lua script (Spring Cloud Gateway's RedisRateLimiter), with limits per route class.

1166
01:08:57.052 --> 01:09:01.553
Return 429 with Retry-After and RateLimit- headers.

1167
01:09:01.833 --> 01:09:07.060
Fail open with a local fallback if Redis fails, and fail closed for abusive classes.

1168
01:09:07.340 --> 01:09:09.132
Hash-tag keys to spread load.

1169
01:09:10.482 --> 01:09:11.378
Architect.

1170
01:09:11.658 --> 01:09:12.853
Question 15.

1171
01:09:13.133 --> 01:09:17.677
Design global traffic management for an India-focused platform with DR.

1172
01:09:17.957 --> 01:09:19.322
Thirty-second answer:

1173
01:09:19.602 --> 01:09:34.834
Anycast CDN or WAF with Indian PoPs terminates TLS near users and routes to the primary region's L7 LB (Mumbai) based on health, with a health-checked failover origin (Hyderabad) for DR.

1174
01:09:35.114 --> 01:09:40.063
DNS points at the CDN, so failover doesn't depend on DNS TTLs.

1175
01:09:40.343 --> 01:09:42.263
Regional stacks are three-AZ.

1176
01:09:43.613 --> 01:09:44.808
Question 16.

1177
01:09:45.088 --> 01:09:49.568
API gateway versus service mesh: how do you divide responsibilities?

1178
01:09:49.848 --> 01:09:51.213
Thirty-second answer:

1179
01:09:51.493 --> 01:10:01.157
Gateway (north-south): client-facing auth, API keys, rate limits, quotas, API versioning, WAF integration and public routing.

1180
01:10:01.437 --> 01:10:14.003
Mesh (east-west): mTLS workload identity, service-to-service authorization, retries and timeouts (one layer!), outlier detection, telemetry and traffic shifting for internal canaries.

1181
01:10:15.353 --> 01:10:16.697
Question 17.

1182
01:10:16.977 --> 01:10:19.515
How do you run progressive delivery at the edge?

1183
01:10:19.795 --> 01:10:21.161
Thirty-second answer:

1184
01:10:21.441 --> 01:10:23.702
Deploy a canary version beside stable.

1185
01:10:23.982 --> 01:10:31.982
Route internal testers by header, then a small weighted slice (1 to 5 %), ideally by user hash for consistency.

1186
01:10:32.262 --> 01:10:41.947
Compare canary versus stable SLOs (errors, latency, business metrics) automatically, and promote in steps or roll back instantly by weight.

1187
01:10:42.227 --> 01:10:44.766
Feature flags separate deploy from release.

1188
01:10:46.116 --> 01:10:47.289
Question 18.

1189
01:10:47.569 --> 01:10:50.641
Zone-aware routing: when does it help, and when does it hurt?

1190
01:10:50.921 --> 01:10:52.287
Thirty-second answer:

1191
01:10:52.567 --> 01:10:58.817
It helps by cutting latency and cross-AZ cost when callers and capacity are balanced across zones.

1192
01:10:59.097 --> 01:11:08.015
It hurts when callers are skewed (all traffic in one zone overloads it), when a zone is partially degraded, or when autoscaling is uneven.

1193
01:11:08.295 --> 01:11:10.513
Always include health and load spill-over.

1194
01:11:11.863 --> 01:11:13.101
Question 19.

1195
01:11:13.381 --> 01:11:15.685
The gateway is a single point of failure.

1196
01:11:15.965 --> 01:11:17.415
How do you make it not one?

1197
01:11:17.695 --> 01:11:19.061
Thirty-second answer:

1198
01:11:19.341 --> 01:11:23.863
Run many stateless pods across AZs behind a redundant cloud LB.

1199
01:11:24.143 --> 01:11:31.183
Keep gateway state external (Redis for limits, with fallbacks) and config in GitOps with validation and canaries.

1200
01:11:31.463 --> 01:11:42.215
Pre-scale for events, shed load by route priority, avoid heavy synchronous dependencies (auth keys cached), and have a break-glass bypass for critical internal traffic.

1201
01:11:43.565 --> 01:11:44.675
Question 20.

1202
01:11:44.955 --> 01:11:51.056
Spring Cloud Gateway versus Envoy-based gateway versus a managed cloud gateway: how do you choose?

1203
01:11:51.336 --> 01:11:52.701
Thirty-second answer:

1204
01:11:52.981 --> 01:11:54.368
Spring Cloud Gateway:

1205
01:11:54.648 --> 01:11:59.469
Java-coded policies, Spring Security, fits Java teams, and you own scaling.

1206
01:11:59.749 --> 01:12:13.211
Envoy-based: high performance, rich balancing (P2C, outlier detection, zone-aware) built in, a polyglot platform, and mesh alignment, but config and extension (Wasm or Lua) skills are needed.

1207
01:12:13.491 --> 01:12:18.141
Managed: least ops, but per-request cost at scale, feature limits, and lock-in.

1208
01:12:19.491 --> 01:12:20.387
Part 9.

1209
01:12:20.667 --> 01:12:22.075
Summary and Practice.

1210
01:12:23.325 --> 01:12:24.413
Key takeaways.

1211
01:12:24.693 --> 01:12:38.197
The edge chain (DNS or anycast, then CDN or WAF, then L7 LB, then gateway, then services) decides where every request goes, how long it waits, and whether it's retried or allowed.

1212
01:12:38.477 --> 01:12:42.189
L4 balances connections, L7 balances requests.

1213
01:12:42.469 --> 01:12:49.296
Long-lived HTTP or 2 or gRPC connections need L7 or client-side balancing.

1214
01:12:49.576 --> 01:12:54.675
Equal-share algorithms (round-robin, random) break on slow-but-healthy backends.

1215
01:12:54.955 --> 01:13:01.269
Use P2C with latency (peak-EWMA) and passive outlier ejection capped at 50 %.

1216
01:13:02.619 --> 01:13:04.667
Liveness never checks dependencies.

1217
01:13:04.947 --> 01:13:06.718
Readiness is instance-local.

1218
01:13:06.998 --> 01:13:08.641
Probes alone miss slow pods.

1219
01:13:08.921 --> 01:13:10.755
Avoid sticky sessions for state.

1220
01:13:11.035 --> 01:13:14.235
Use consistent hashing only for performance affinity.

1221
01:13:14.515 --> 01:13:19.657
Retries: idempotent only, a different instance, one layer, jittered, within a budget.

1222
01:13:19.937 --> 01:13:23.009
Writes are retried by clients with idempotency keys.

1223
01:13:23.289 --> 01:13:27.491
The gateway strips then sets identity headers from validated tokens.

1224
01:13:27.771 --> 01:13:32.785
Services must be reachable only through the gateway or mesh, and they still check ownership.

1225
01:13:34.135 --> 01:13:39.596
Per-route timeouts from latency budgets, and streams exempted by explicit, ordered routes.

1226
01:13:39.876 --> 01:13:44.825
Zone-aware routing needs load-aware spill-over, or skewed callers overload a zone.

1227
01:13:45.105 --> 01:13:51.484
Global failover belongs at an anycast edge with health-checked origins, not in DNS TTLs.

